   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 30739849,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 3322973,
    "poster_id": 571016,
    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/4/f5424dbee8507d9e9265cb3589e3f3f85a136338.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1A_2...A_{99}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"17\" > be a regular <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/7/1f714a7b19af80f507299e77ab380e23ab003474.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$99-$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"34\" height=\"15\" >g</span>on and point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/3/3b349ce6072b7d382ab8e0f6bece9f081f85425b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" > be its center. find the smallest possible natural number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" > , such that Parsa can color all segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/7/e6709958d970625f2504210e8cbde672f1e328da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_iA_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"17\" > ( <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/8/76882afc60ea418ee8696fe3489ee084dbf9006d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\le i &lt; j \\le 100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"123\" height=\"16\" > ) with one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" > colors in such a way that no two homochromatic segments intersect each other or share a vertex.<br>\n<br>\n<i>Proposed by Josef Tkadlec - Czech Republic</i>",
    "post_canonical": "Let $A_1A_2...A_{99}$ be a regular $99-$gon and point $A_{100}$ be its center. find the smallest possible natural number $n$ , such that Parsa can color all segments $A_iA_j$ ( $1 \\le i < j \\le 100$ ) with one of $n$ colors in such a way that no two homochromatic segments intersect each other or share a vertex.\n\n[i]Proposed by Josef Tkadlec - Czech Republic[/i]",
